00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.740
So Cloud Armor is
deployed actually

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.490
at the edge of Google's
global network.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.972
It's part of our globe
balancing infrastructure.

00:01:56.972 --> 00:01:58.680
Before I begin, I do
want to kind of give

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.020
an overview of what our
network looks like to give you

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.850
an idea of the scale and scope.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.560
And it goes a long
way in explaining

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.380
in how we're able to do
the protections that we

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.110
are able to do.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.500
So we actually have
19 regions or data

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.240
centers distributed globally.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.040
You can see here in blue.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.930
We also have a future
ones coming in,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.380
and those are the white dots.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.920
In addition, we have
another 134 edge POPs,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.780
or points of presence.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.040
These are co-located facilities
within our ISP partners' data

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.070
centers.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.690
And what that means is we're
able to receive client traffic

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.520
and put them on
the Google network

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.530
as close as possible
to the clients.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.400
So once a request is
made to a Google IP,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.733
it gets routed to
the nearest edge POP,

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.150
and then it's on
Google's backbone

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.120
instead of being routed
through the public internet.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.492
Speaking of the backbone,
we have 13 subsea cable

00:02:54.492 --> 00:02:56.700
investments that are either
wholly or partially owned

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.380
by us.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:57.880
All right.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.400
Those are all the blue lines
that are being strung together.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.100
On a daily basis, we move--

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.130
the amount of data that we
move across our backbone

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.770
is orders of magnitude
larger than the data that

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.750
traverses the public internet.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.570
What that means is
Google's network capacity

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.760
is so large that we are
actually able to effectively

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.150
absorb and dissipate most of
the most common distributed

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.480
denial of service
attacks without impacting

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.010
any availability or reliability.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.887
So most of our
customers don't even

00:03:27.887 --> 00:03:29.220
know that they've been attacked.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.540
Like on other clouds, security
is a shared model on GCP.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.670
And when it comes to network
security and security

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.310
in general, we work
to secure our networks

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.410
and infrastructure, and
we provide all the tools

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.910
and capabilities
for customers to do

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:49.070
the same in their environments.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:50.920
In fact, we enable and
encourage customers

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.590
to follow a defense
in depth approach,

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.800
where you deploy various
security solutions

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.080
at various stages of the
stack that you can see here--

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.350
oh, I'm kind of in the way.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.810
And at every point
in the stack, we also

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.030
have room for partner
solutions to deliver

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.730
capabilities that may be
more specialized than we

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.040
offer natively.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.640
Today we'll be talking about
Cloud Armor, up at the top end,

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.920
for application protection
as well as our global load

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.672
balancing infrastructure.

00:04:19.672 --> 00:04:21.339
But I will start off
with kind of giving

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:24.680
an overview of the network
security controls that we have.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:25.390
All right.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.510
So we have three different types
of network security controls

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.150
that customers often
deploy in unison.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.680
In red, we've got VPC firewalls.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.550
These are your traditional
firewalls using

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.530
layer 3, layer 4 semantics.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:42.550
You would use these to define
the perimeter of a VPC,

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.260
north, south, as
well as east, west,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.930
but you can even get
as granular as per VM.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.920
So you can use VPC firewalls
to control and define ACLs

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.740
on a per VM basis if necessary.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.110
They have an analog in blue
called VPC Service Controls.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.190
VPC Service Controls allow
you to define effectively

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.960
a virtual perimeter
around GCP-based services

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.550
like managed APIs, things like
Google Cloud Storage, BigTable,

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:10.720
BigQuery.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.690
The point is you can use VPC
Service Controls to ensure

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.710
that only authorized users
only from authorized locations

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.770
using only authorized
machines can access or receive

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.260
the data within those
API-based services

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.690
to mitigate things
like data exfiltration

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.250
and really just make sure that
only permitted functions are

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.370
executing on your resources.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.120
And finally, in green,
we've got Cloud Armor,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.180
which is instrumented in part
of our global load balancing

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.390
infrastructure.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.350
And that's able to
filter and drop traffic

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.230
coming from the public
internet into GCP.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.970
So as I'll dive in
a little deeper,

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:52.750
we're able to do layer 3
through layer 7 filtering.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.220
And I'll kind explain
how that works.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:56.720
Yeah.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.840
Before doing that, understanding
how our global load balancing

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.860
infrastructure
works is valuable.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:13.680
So the GCLB is not a DNS-based
global load balancer.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.460
As many of you know, we actually
publish in any cast VIP.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.560
And this virtual IP is
accessible from anywhere

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.650
in the world.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:23.260
And when a client coming
from California, or New York,

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:27.750
or Singapore goes and
accesses that same IP,

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.630
the edge POP location
nearest to them

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.490
will receive that
traffic, all right,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.080
and will then filter
that traffic--

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.930
will enter that traffic onto
the Google's network, where

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.080
it will be routed to the
nearest region wherever

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.970
your applications
may be deployed.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.242
So if you only have one--

00:06:43.242 --> 00:06:45.450
your application is only
hosted in one region, that's

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.680
where the traffic will go.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.090
But if you have
your applications

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.280
across different regions
distributed globally,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:56.490
then we will route the
traffic where necessary.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.200
So our load balancers
are SSL terminating.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:02.700
All right.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.750
And what that means is
customers can do SSL offload.

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.810
You upload your certificates
into your global load balancer

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:13.785
instance, and then we decrypt
the traffic on the way in,

00:07:13.785 --> 00:07:15.410
and then we'll
re-encrypt it on the way

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.850
back out to your back end.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.680
But in that small
sliver of space

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:20.210
where it is in
plain text, that's

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.060
where Cloud Armor is actually
able to do the inspection

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.460
and evaluate and
drop the traffic.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.480
Similarly, other
points in the stack

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.860
will filter out and drop
bad traffic, however

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:31.610
that's defined.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:35.317
Layer 3, layer 4
attacks, malformed

00:07:35.317 --> 00:07:37.400
packets and things like
that will get scrubbed out

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.590
at various stages of
the stack, all of which

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:42.410
happens way upstream of
the customer back ends.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.720
So as I said, Cloud Armor is
a part of our load balancing

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.970
infrastructure.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.880
Together with HTTP load
balancer and Cloud Armor,

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.920
customers are
automatically protected

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.160
from the most common types of
distributed denial of service

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.810
attacks, things like SYN floods,
ACK floods, DNS amplifications.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.880
These are the types of attacks
that hit the news most common,

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.940
most often.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.060
These are the ones that have
terabyte level bandwidth usage

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.390
and things like that.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:17.810
And all that is protected
and blocked automatically.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.560
Like I said earlier,
most of our customers

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.710
don't even know that
they've been attacked.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.850
And we are working on
exposing that visibility,

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:28.380
so in the future you will
be able to get a report

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:33.760
and do sort of after-action
investigation as necessary.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.679
Cloud Armor proper, as I said,
we are in the sliver of space

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:40.580
where the traffic
is in plain text,

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:43.480
so we can do full
layer 7 inspection.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.750
We don't look at the body, but
we do look at all the requests

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.330
headers and the cookies,
so you're actually

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:53.740
able to define ACLs
today with IPs, right?

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.280
We were generally available
for IP allow and deny.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:00.220
But in the future,
currently in an alpha,

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.610
we've got geo-based access
control, a full set of WAF

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:06.610
capabilities, as well as
a custom rules language

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.310
to allow you to
define your own rules.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:17.180
And all of this should
go without saying,

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:19.120
but it's good to repeat it.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.130
All of this is built on
top of infrastructure

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.950
that we have designed and built
for Google over the past 20

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.908
years to protect against
these common attacks

00:09:26.908 --> 00:09:28.450
to make sure that
things like search,

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:34.097
and ads, and Gmail, and YouTube
all stay up and available.

00:09:34.097 --> 00:09:35.680
So it's that same
expertise that we've

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.290
developed protecting our
own applications that

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.690
allows us to go and
enable customers

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:43.910
to leverage that investment.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:44.900
Very big numbers there.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:51.075
So as I mentioned,
Cloud Armor is deployed

00:09:51.075 --> 00:09:52.200
at the edge of the network.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.700
And what that means
is it's actually

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.180
able to enforce the
security policies upstream

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:59.560
without consuming
your own compute.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:01.730
So Cloud Armor enables
customers to protect

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:06.200
applications from DDoS, filter
incoming requests by Geo,

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:10.070
as well as most
layer 7 parameters.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.870
And as a web
application firewall,

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.200
we're also going to
ship pre-canned rules

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.650
to protect against the web's
kind of most common application

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:19.625
attacks.

00:10:19.625 --> 00:10:22.130
So we are porting over
the mod security core

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.510
rule set, if you're
familiar with what that is.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.110
And we're starting with SQL
injection cross-site scripting,

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:34.190
but the plan is to port over and
support the full core rule set.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.320
And critically, we also
have real-time telemetry

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.750
for your own monitoring
and security needs.

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:41.160
I'll show what that looks
like a little bit later.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.510
But the point is you've got
logs going to Stackdriver,

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:47.100
and you've also got a
monitoring dashboard

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.300
that helps you detect and react
when there is something to do.

00:10:53.763 --> 00:10:55.930
So as I mentioned, the Cloud
Armor security policies

00:10:55.930 --> 00:11:00.730
are used to customize access
to protected resources.

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.580
And it could be as flexible
and as uniform or granular

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:04.503
as you need.

00:11:04.503 --> 00:11:05.920
So although the
protection happens

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.290
at the very edge
of the network, you

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.625
can define the security policy--

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.460
define access controls that
could be applied consistently

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.340
to all of your applications
in a single project.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:20.800
There's kind of a one-to-many
relationship between policy

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.420
and backend service.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.550
Or, if the business
dictates, you

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.350
can actually have
customized controls

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:28.180
where you would
have one security

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.070
policy per backend service.

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:33.940
And all that matters is
what you would allow or deny

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:37.840
into that application.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.850
Once traffic comes into
a backend service that

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:42.830
is protected by a
security policy,

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:46.300
the rules within that policy
are evaluated in priority order.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:51.730
Much like most application
security appliances out there,

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:54.140
although Cloud Armor
is not an appliance,

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:57.550
we fall down the list of
rules in priority order.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.480
Each rule has a match condition.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:02.290
And if traffic matches
that match condition,

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.840
then the action associated
with that rule will be taken.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.750
The action can be allow, deny.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.500
In the future, the
action will be throttle

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:12.000
if you need to do
some rate limiting.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.770
And if none of the traffic
matches, if none of the rules

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.840
hit, then the default
action will be taken.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:18.610
And, again, that
could be allow, deny,

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:20.360
or throttle in the future.

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:24.050
And that provides the ability
to create very flexible,

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:25.627
very granular controls.

00:12:25.627 --> 00:12:27.460
One best practice
recommendation, of course,

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:29.650
is, since we
terminate evaluation

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.140
after the first match,
it's best to have

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.590
all of your deny rules higher
priority than your allow rules.

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:37.840
So if you do need to drop
traffic, that happens first.

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.250
In terms of overall
features and status--

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.220
so I've mentioned this
as I've talked before,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.690
but we do have a rich and
comprehensive feature set.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:49.520
Some of it is generally
available today.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:53.210
We announced GA back
at RSA last month

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.340
for the granular
policy framework

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.130
of the overall distributed
denial of service protection,

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:01.730
the layer 3, layer 4 stuff,
as well as the ability

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:05.510
to create IP lists to
allow or deny traffic based

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.490
on the IP in those policies.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.850
That's GA, as is all
of our telemetry.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.590
Currently in alpha
we have the rest

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:14.210
of the WAF slate of features.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:16.820
That includes Geo
access controls,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.220
that includes our
custom rules language

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:25.190
on top of which we've built
[INAUDIBLE] security rules.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:28.108
SQL injection and cross-site
scripting are up first.

00:13:28.108 --> 00:13:30.650
Shortly thereafter, we'll follow
with things like remote code

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:38.500
execution, local file inclusion,
and remote file inclusion,

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:39.860
and then customer rules.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:42.860
So in addition to us shipping
these pre-baked rules,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.620
customers will be able
to use our rules language

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:48.860
to select on any layer
3 through layer 7

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:51.830
attribute of the
traffic in order

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.290
to filter out based
on business needs.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.890
So now going over some
of the most typical

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.170
use cases that we've got.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:06.830
As I mentioned before, we've
got DDoS protection, right?

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:09.110
That's first and foremost.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.990
You get that as soon as you
deploy behind the HTTP load

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.090
balancer in Cloud Armor.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:18.200
The idea is we will
filter out and scrub out

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.870
any bad layer 3, layer
4 traffic to the extent

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:24.470
that you're only serving
HTTP or TCP traffic,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.870
and only good requests
will get to you.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:28.370
All right.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:29.900
So this is very common.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:32.300
Most customers love this.

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:35.630
And you'll hear from
relays in eBay talking

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:38.750
about their thoughts on this.

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:41.310
Here's an example of our real
time monitoring dashboard.

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:41.810
All right.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.870
So we provide
granular visibility

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:48.740
both at an overall level about
all your incoming traffic

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:51.920
and the percentages and the
actual numbers of what's

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:54.830
allowed and what's blocked.

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.810
But critically, we
also have the ability

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.570
to deploy rules in preview mode.

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:05.780
So this is akin to sort of
passive rules in older WAF

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:06.620
deployments.

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.500
The idea is you would model
the impact of the rule

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:11.150
without it actually having--

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:14.300
without it taking action
on your production traffic.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.610
So we log rules in preview mode.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.040
They log their action
to Stackdriver,

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.715
but they don't actually
stop the traffic.

00:15:21.715 --> 00:15:23.090
And then it's up
to you to decide

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:24.870
to take it off preview mode.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:28.332
So you're able to monitor
the impact of previewed rules

00:15:28.332 --> 00:15:30.290
in these managed monitoring
dashboards as well.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:31.920
You can visualize it.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.290
And most importantly,
you can set up

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.622
custom alerting policies.

00:15:36.622 --> 00:15:39.080
Using any of the metrics that
are exposed in the monitoring

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:42.290
dashboards, you would set
up custom alerting policies

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:46.058
to trigger any of your
incident response processes,

00:15:46.058 --> 00:15:47.600
notify the right
people, and then you

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:48.620
can go and take action.

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:57.648
One of the most common use
cases is restricting source IPs.

00:15:57.648 --> 00:15:59.190
Many of our customers
have a business

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:03.660
need to filter their traffic
through an upstream proxy

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:06.750
and yet still want to have
their projects in GCP.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:10.470
And the HTTP load balancer will
expose a publicly available IP

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:11.560
address.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.920
So what they'll do is they'll
use Cloud Armor to define

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:17.850
a default deny all rule and
then create two higher priority

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.520
rules that would
allow the source

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:23.460
IPs of their upstream
proxy and perhaps

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:25.680
their own corporate
range of IPs.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:28.560
And the effect would be
then that only traffic

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.870
from the upstream proxy is
allowed through the load

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:33.390
balancer to the
backend services.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.190
Meanwhile, anyone
else from the internet

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.700
that hits the public IP
directly would get denied.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:40.200
All right.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.570
That's only if you
need to force traffic

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:46.420
to come through
an upstream proxy.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:48.240
And the final use
case I want to talk

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.810
about today is the idea
of downstream detection

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:53.740
and upstream enforcement.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:56.130
So the way that
this generally works

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:59.670
is customers will deploy some
basic Cloud Armor security

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:00.580
policies.

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:01.080
All right.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.390
And they'll have
their applications

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.790
within GCP as backend services.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.200
And they also have
their own sort

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:13.410
of custom monitoring solutions.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.240
They're doing monitoring
and analytics,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.460
looking at the application
logs, but they're also

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:19.680
bringing in data from a lot
of other sources, like--

00:17:23.407 --> 00:17:24.859
There we go.

00:17:24.859 --> 00:17:26.720
They're bringing data
from other sources,

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:29.870
maybe the load balancer
logs, maybe anything else

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:33.160
going to their own [INAUDIBLE],,
any events going to their

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:34.490
[? SIMs, ?] things like that.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:39.230
On top of that they'll have
threat or fraud detection

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.600
algorithms running,
or abuse detection,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:44.450
and any number of things that
they'll identify something

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:46.820
that's bad based
on things that are

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.430
unique to their
applications, taking

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.560
all the context necessary.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.480
And then they'll
define a signature

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.600
and then push that signature
as a Cloud Armor rule upstream.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:58.760
And the idea is,
once you've decided

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.300
that some set of traffic is
bad, you can go and enforce that

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:04.820
upstream in Cloud
Armor, and that you

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:07.340
would have near real-time
propagation measured

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:15.020
in minutes to globally enforce
these security policies.

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:17.840
With that, I'd like to
hand it off to Tzury,

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:21.890
invite him back on stage,
to talk about Reblaze

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:25.892
and their integration with GCP.

00:18:25.892 --> 00:18:26.784
[APPLAUSE]

