So before diving
into my slides, I'd

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:40.910
like to take this
opportunity to thank

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:48.580
you both, the team at Google
and at eBay, for your trust--

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:52.640
first of all, for your trust and
for this great amazing journey

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.430
we did together in the
last several months.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:02.420
So Reblaze is a comprehensive
security platform,

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:04.640
or to be more precise,
comprehensive application

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:10.700
security platform
deployed in the cloud.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:15.260
We're about to explore Reblaze,
the features of Reblaze,

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:19.220
as well as how Reblaze
integrated into GCP

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:24.080
leverages GCP products and
platforms Cloud Armor already

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:29.300
mentioned, but also a few other
products such as Cloud Security

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:34.800
Command Center, BigQuery,
and Machine Learning.

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.080
Reblaze is a cloud
native solution

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.400
that provides you with nearly
everything you need to control

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.650
and command your application.

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:51.690
When we're looking at
today's threats map,

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.800
we think it will be
wise to divide it

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.500
into four different pillars--

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:03.460
DDoS, well-known,
common on a daily basis,

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:08.290
bot management, which is
basically a subset of DDoS,

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.370
or it shows some
similarities with DDoS

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:17.760
in terms of automated traffic,
runs across a large network.

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:22.440
But instead of
flooding your network,

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:27.630
flooding your resources with
incoming traffic, bot detection

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:30.000
solution, bot
management solution

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.040
will protect your
platform from attacks

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:39.420
such as account take over, brute
force logging, even credit card

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.830
fraud, and simple
scraping, data scraping.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:50.700
If your business is your
data and vise versa,

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.580
scraping is an issue
you have to deal

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:58.080
with literally every minute.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:02.340
The next is web
application firewall.

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:04.560
While DDoS and
bot management are

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:07.330
focusing on traffic,
behaviors and patterns

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:12.130
within traffic, what
application firewall focuses on,

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:16.600
the data, the actual data
submitted over to the server.

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:21.240
Does the data contain any
malicious vectors, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:26.250
SQL injections, [INAUDIBLE]
file inclusions, et cetera?

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:32.190
And last but not least,
especially nowadays,

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.650
API security.

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:39.810
And one might think, what makes
API security a special case?

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.930
While it probably
can be described

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:47.030
as yet just another web
application platform,

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:50.730
it's just a simple HTTP
request back and forth,

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:55.450
reading data and
responding to the clients.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:22:01.180
However, today's
API, or as they're

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:06.490
called modern APIs, while
originally were designed

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:09.310
to enable computers,
service, communicate

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:15.520
with each other, today's API,
by design, serving humans,

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:19.490
and bots, and machines
at the same time.

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:23.650
If you have an API and you
built your web application

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:28.950
with Angular, or React,
or any other framework,

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:32.170
the user interface, the
interacting within a browser

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:37.980
or within a mobile device, is
actually using API underneath.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:41.650
And the very same
API will be used

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:46.380
when you will release your
mobile native application.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:49.300
And at the same time,
your clients or affiliates

00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:52.570
will use the same set
of API to communicate

00:22:52.570 --> 00:22:56.440
and to transfer data between
their service and yours.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:23:02.020
So with Reblaze, one of the
key strengths of Reblaze

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:04.210
is enabling you
and, in most cases,

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:08.050
automatically profile
each and every case

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:13.280
and assign the designated
policy optimized for each case.

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:19.000
So human access API will
be enforcing and monitoring

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:23.260
the human behavior along
the line while machines--

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:26.950
B2B API, server to
server API, will enjoy

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:31.840
and will run under different,
completely different policy.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:35.140
Also, by definition, the fact
API is all about transmitting

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:39.170
data back and forth, API is--

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:42.970
eventually, each
operation puts some data,

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:46.230
puts some stream of bytes
either in a database

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.190
or in their drive.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:51.280
And that suggests direct
access to a database somehow,

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:55.620
and therefore, security
became much more critical.

00:23:55.620 --> 00:24:00.080
And last, but from the service
we take from our clients

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:01.220
as well as from--

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:05.030
our analysts are
actually reporting.

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:09.960
The greatest challenge with
API today is, where are they?

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:13.550
So it used to be a [? slash ?]
API or API subdomain

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:17.190
to run your API, but today's
within organizations,

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:21.360
APIs are becoming publicly
available on a daily basis,

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:22.590
literally.

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.630
Every cloud function
you put out,

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:28.820
every microservice you release
or you consume and utilize,

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:30.690
that's actually in APIs.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:34.590
So for the system administrator,
for the security engineer

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:37.980
within an organization,
there is no way

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:42.177
they actually know and familiar
with the location and purpose

00:24:42.177 --> 00:24:44.760
of each and every API, because
developers are simply releasing

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:45.860
them on a daily basis.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:54.560
So let's talk about
the setup of Reblaze

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:56.810
and its relations
and relationship

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:01.770
with Cloud Armor and other
cloud security products.

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:05.330
So in a typical GCP
product, you will have--

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:08.160
and Emil has actually
covered some of it.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:11.520
So you will have a
global CDN in front.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.270
The global CDN will be
working in conjunction

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:19.640
with a global balancer and
in GCP and Cloud Armor.

00:25:19.640 --> 00:25:23.930
So CDN will be handling
content delivery

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:27.020
in a most efficient way.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:29.330
Load balancer will
handle everything

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:36.650
related to SSL termination and
ensuring high availability.

00:25:36.650 --> 00:25:40.680
And Cloud Armor is processing
and handling the security.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:43.550
So you have those three
products and platforms actually

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:48.860
becoming your front shield
and your front shield

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:54.150
for your global public
front-end for your traffic.

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:58.140
Now, Reblaze stands
right next to it.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:01.980
So instead of a load balancer
communicating with your scale

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:06.240
group, with your
[INAUDIBLE] directly,

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:09.320
the load balancer will
communicate with Reblaze.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:17.000
This Reblaze enforces a
DDoS, block all the bots,

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.700
operates with the web
application firewall,

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.300
and provides the API security.

00:26:25.255 --> 00:26:28.320
How long Reblaze
does in real time,

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:32.030
protecting, blocking,
dropping, or letting in?

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:36.810
Reblaze pushes all
the data to BigQuery.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:37.890
It's not real time.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:41.150
It's about 10 seconds,
10 to 12 seconds delay,

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.340
but it's nearly real time.

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:46.960
Now, this is where all the
fun stuff are happening,

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.270
because BigQuery and Reblaze
is being continuously

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:52.760
analyzed, used to
analyze the data,

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:56.960
and every incoming
request and every session

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.710
be analyzed continuously.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:03.590
And as we detect attacks
or misbehave from a user,

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:07.790
from a session, attacks of which
you cannot block it based upon

00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:09.950
a given request, rather
you need some track record,

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:12.350
you need to see a pattern.

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:17.990
As we detect an attack
source, Reblaze proxies

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:21.950
immediately get updated
as well as Cloud Armor

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:24.790
get updated immediately.

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:28.910
So there is immediate
action taking place

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:32.600
as attacks are detected.

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:41.540
This setup, the way we set it
up enabled us to provide you

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:48.610
the users with four features,
or some call it principles,

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:52.630
which we believe
are key requirements

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:57.130
in every security solution.

00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:59.710
The one is absolute visibility.

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:01.120
That is a given.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.980
You cannot protect against
something you're not seeing.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:10.996
In a web scale, when you
process millions or billions

00:28:10.996 --> 00:28:14.920
of requests a day, you
have to have the ability

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:19.330
to facilitate the tools
to analyze and get

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:23.490
to each and every record and
track each and every session.

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:28.990
But at Reblaze, we do more
than just data analysis

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:31.030
in real time.

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:33.640
The second principle
is immediate action.

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:38.850
As I mentioned, action
taken in every request.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:41.950
Farther processes are
dropped immediately.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:46.080
We detect the source of the
attack, update immediately.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:49.300
Proxies update immediately
to Cloud Armor.

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:57.520
Not only that, we also have
integrated with Google--

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:02.920
other [? SIM ?] products called
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:29:02.920 --> 00:29:07.390
So also, we push alerts,
and we push all the events

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:11.050
to your command center so you
can get, in one screen, all

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:12.770
the recent attacks.

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:15.520
And if an event is
taking place, you'll

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:17.020
be able to take
action immediately

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:18.140
straight from the portal.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:26.990
The third feature
is recommendation.

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:29.520
We help do the thinking for you.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:33.350
It's really hard to keep
in mind all the areas,

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:35.660
all the policies
required, all the changes

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.150
within your application.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:40.600
As I'll show you soon, Reblaze
provides a recommendation

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:42.830
where security policies
might be better

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:47.150
off fine tune, restricted
access, API discovery,

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:48.800
as I explained.

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:50.970
Nobody knows today
where their API is.

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:56.140
Reblaze, given the capacity and
the availability of BigQuery,

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:59.150
every day we analyze
the entire traffic,

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:03.350
and we spot errors in which
we detected API activity.

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:06.290
And we say, OK,
here's an API section

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:08.450
within your application.

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:10.640
It is not profiled
yet as an API.

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:13.430
Would you like to in
one click set it as API?

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:19.850
So you will enjoy all the
capabilities of API security.

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:24.930
And last is our biometric
behavioral analysis.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:32.160
So this is a capability
which, in the recent months,

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:40.560
became the tool, the
tool that we are actually

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:45.630
fighting and detecting the
most sophisticated bots.

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:49.050
And the biometric-- I will
just be explaining to you

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:49.690
how it works.

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:54.240
So in addition to the
session graph that we hold

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:58.410
and process-- in Reblaze,
it's per user for each user--

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:01.200
there is another graph
which we maintain,

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:04.980
and that is the human
interaction, the human presence

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:06.770
along this session.

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:09.840
So its applications are
protected by Reblaze.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:13.110
Every click and
every keyboard tap--

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.800
every click on the keyboard,
every tapping, scrolling,

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.370
every event that happens
on the client side

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:23.370
is monitored and
reported to Reblaze

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:25.540
and analyzed in BigQuery.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:27.930
So when we look at the data,
when we look at-- when we

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:30.600
inspect and analyze a session
and each and every session get

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:31.680
analyzed--

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:36.120
not only that we have all the
data and all the transaction

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:40.300
performed by this session,
we also have, was there

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:42.160
a human at all?

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:45.540
Do we actually-- are we
dealing with a human, or just

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:52.590
emulated or headless browser,
or any type of sophisticated bot

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:54.090
attacks?

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:57.270
And that wouldn't be possible
without the BigQuery.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:00.360
Because given we're
processing billions, literally

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:04.610
billions and billions
of requests a day,

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:08.280
BigQuery gives us the
ability to add on top of it

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:11.670
several other billions of
mouse clicks and keyboards

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:18.600
and yet provides you as a user,
and our platforms, our systems,

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:22.830
immediate analysis and
immediate information

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:24.120
about each and every session.

00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:29.690
When we talk about
visibility, if I

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:31.505
may take a few more
seconds to expand

00:32:31.505 --> 00:32:34.920
the visibility of Reblaze--

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:41.960
So in addition to the fact that
every request, every header,

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:46.580
every cookie, every
authentication

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:51.800
action, every API call
is stored in Reblaze

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:59.240
and analyzed in Reblaze,
we enrich every request

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:04.880
with metadata, which gives
us a better profiling of each

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:05.630
and every session.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:09.440
For instance, every request
in Reblaze is marked.

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:13.750
Is it a human, or headless
browser, or a bot?

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:17.930
Is this session operating
within the Tor network,

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:20.780
or behind a proxy,
or behind a VPN?

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.860
Is it a server on a VPS on
a cloud somewhere or a data

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:24.920
center?

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:30.620
Or is it a domestic
office link connection?

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:33.150
And those profiles-- and
so absolute visibility

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:37.590
means all this data is always
available to make decision,

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:39.300
to take action when needed.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:49.800
Another case which BigQuery
became critical for us

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:54.880
is a bot detecting brute force
attacks, such as loggings

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:55.800
or account takeover.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:34:03.410
For instance, anybody
recognize what this is?

00:34:03.410 --> 00:34:07.890
So this is one of the
things we found by working

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:09.409
with Derek and his team.

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:11.760
Basically, as you
probably-- if you

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:19.050
know, with Gmail, your mailbox,
on the left to the @ sign,

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.500
you can spell it however you
want with any number of dots,

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:23.310
any position.

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:26.219
It will always be
the same inbox.

00:34:26.219 --> 00:34:28.440
So if you have a
mechanism that says,

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.570
validate your account
by sending you an email,

00:34:30.570 --> 00:34:31.820
and you'll link on--

00:34:31.820 --> 00:34:35.880
you go, you click on the
link, and that's all.

00:34:35.880 --> 00:34:37.753
Your account is validated.

00:34:37.753 --> 00:34:40.170
What we have found in this
case, in this particular case--

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:42.850
and there's thousands
of cases like this--

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:46.889
we found 697 different
variations of this email,

00:34:46.889 --> 00:34:48.389
as you saw.

00:34:48.389 --> 00:34:52.530
Now, those cases, we
found them by mistake.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:56.699
We had no plan to go and seek
for this particular attack.

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:59.070
But given the machine
learning and the analysis

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:01.720
around BigQuery
continuously, the algorithm

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:04.200
detected too much
of a similarity

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:06.485
between hundreds of
hundreds of accounts

00:35:06.485 --> 00:35:08.310
and said, take a look at this.

00:35:08.310 --> 00:35:11.410
We spot something.

00:35:11.410 --> 00:35:14.730
So Reblaze has more
features than that,

00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:17.760
but time doesn't
allow us to do so.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:21.150
And this case, I will
hand over to Derek

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:24.270
so he can share with us
his experience with Reblaze

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:25.781
and Cloud Armor.

00:35:25.781 --> 00:35:27.805
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:27.805 --> 00:35:29.180
DEREK CHAMORRO:
Thank you, Tzury.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:31.150
So first I want to
thank our legal team

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:33.905
for allowing me to talk about
this, because in most cases

00:35:33.905 --> 00:35:34.780
we're not allowed to.

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:37.310
So some of you might be
wondering why I'm up here.

00:35:37.310 --> 00:35:39.880
Given the nature of eBay, we
kind of build our own servers

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:43.090
and we put them all around
our global data centers.

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:45.730
But like most of you,
I like to be prepared.

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:47.835
As a security
professional, too, I'm

00:35:47.835 --> 00:35:49.210
extremely paranoid
about whatever

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:50.830
the next big attack is.

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:53.230
And when I think about
some of our brands

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:56.240
potentially going into an
area that we don't own,

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.570
and if we don't have a
proper strategy in place,

00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:01.670
then that potentially is
a threat vector for us.

00:36:01.670 --> 00:36:04.720
So part of this use
case was to determine,

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:08.590
how could we replicate our
existing known perimeter

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:10.520
security controls
into an environment

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:12.940
that essentially we don't know?

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:15.670
So some of you might
think of-- or know eBay

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:19.330
as being just eBay.com.

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:23.110
And it's an online marketplace
with over 175 million users,

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:26.750
with over 1.1 billion
live listings at any time.

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:28.240
But I like to think
of eBay as kind

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:30.430
of like the roof of a house.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:32.620
And under that roof,
we have many brands

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:35.710
that fit our marketplace
strategy, whether it

00:36:35.710 --> 00:36:38.380
be StubHub, which is the
world's largest online ticket

00:36:38.380 --> 00:36:41.355
marketplace, or a lot of
our other global brands

00:36:41.355 --> 00:36:42.730
that help people
around the world

00:36:42.730 --> 00:36:45.550
find what they're looking for
in their local communities.

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:47.720
So what do they all
share in common?

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:50.118
Well, we as a security team,
we treat them all equally.

00:36:50.118 --> 00:36:51.910
And we want to ensure
that we're protecting

00:36:51.910 --> 00:36:56.200
not only their brand image
but their reputation.

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:57.950
So some of the goals
that we had with this

00:36:57.950 --> 00:36:59.870
is that we wanted
to first experiment

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:03.170
with how we could extend
this perimeter protection

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:07.040
strategy no matter where
our data could live.

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:08.930
Second, we wanted
to kind of preserve

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:11.490
the same level of visibility
that we currently have

00:37:11.490 --> 00:37:12.740
within our on-premise systems.

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:14.720
Ideally, we want to
be able to correlate

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:17.048
that data with our
on-premise systems

00:37:17.048 --> 00:37:19.340
with whatever we were able
to achieve in an environment

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:22.580
that essentially we didn't own.

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:25.100
And third, we just wanted to
see if we could break anything.

00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:27.240
I mean, the inert
hacker in all of us,

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:30.240
we want to see where we could
induce some kind of failure,

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:32.660
whether it be within something
we found within Google,

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:33.950
or something we
found within Reblaze,

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.283
or something we found within
the systems we provisioned.

00:37:36.283 --> 00:37:37.790
And on a side
note, when we asked

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:39.720
our customer
engineer, just said,

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:41.640
hey, listen, we
want to DDoS Google,

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:42.930
I expected some kind of delay.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.270
And I was actually
really surprised

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:45.603
when he said, yeah, let's do it.

00:37:45.603 --> 00:37:49.710
So it made me pretty
happy about that.

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:52.363
So a lot of you who have
developed a DDoS security

00:37:52.363 --> 00:37:53.780
program may be
familiar with this.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:55.670
What this is is
part of the approach

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:59.780
of having a successful defensive
strategies to create tooling

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:01.430
for common DDoS threat vectors.

00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:04.070
So we put it across three
pillars-- anomaly detection,

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:06.500
visibility and forensics,
and attack mitigation.

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:09.310
And in most cases, all
of these work together

00:38:09.310 --> 00:38:10.970
to mitigate attacks.

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:13.640
So you might have a
small attack that sneaks

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:14.840
under configured thresholds.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:17.935
So to mitigate that
potential impact,

00:38:17.935 --> 00:38:19.820
you would first
look at some kind

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:21.472
of anomaly detection of alert.

00:38:21.472 --> 00:38:23.930
And with this alert, you would
use your existing visibility

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:26.300
and forensics tools to
determine whether or not

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:29.148
the attack was real
and then look for ways

00:38:29.148 --> 00:38:30.440
to be able to block the attack.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:33.080
And then you would use your
attack mitigation tools

00:38:33.080 --> 00:38:34.840
to implement some kind
of filter in order

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:36.090
to be able to filter that out.

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:37.880
So with this in
mind, we built a set

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:40.070
of requirements that
could mirror the existing

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:42.950
tooling that we had on premise
and do kind of like a feature

00:38:42.950 --> 00:38:44.785
parity comparison
with the existing

00:38:44.785 --> 00:38:46.160
controls available
through Google

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:50.310
as well as through
their trusted partners.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:52.520
So we decided to
build a test site.

00:38:52.520 --> 00:38:54.290
And while the test
setup wasn't a replica

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:56.210
of what our traditional
sites would look like,

00:38:56.210 --> 00:38:57.980
we wanted to build
a project that

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:00.200
would represent all
aspects of what we want

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:01.800
to accomplish from this test.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:04.040
And we discuss actually
building a mirror site,

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:05.510
but what we realized was
that we would probably

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:07.718
blow through our security
budget for the entire year.

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:12.915
So this is an example
of what we had.

00:39:12.915 --> 00:39:14.540
And first, we wanted
to build something

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:15.710
that was fully automated.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:17.510
If we encountered
any failure, we

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:19.460
wanted to just be
able to destroy it

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:21.230
and rebuild it as it is.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:24.730
So it kind of shows a
reflection of the attack traffic

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:25.730
that we would expect in.

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:28.190
So first, on the
right-hand side,

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:30.890
before we engage with
any other third parties,

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:32.733
we decided to build
some attack VMs

00:39:32.733 --> 00:39:35.150
and kind of create this hairpin
traffic that would come in

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:36.110
through the internet.

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:37.693
And it first hit the
Google front-end.

00:39:37.693 --> 00:39:42.320
So we automatically knew that
we had your L3 and L4 protection

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:43.700
that was managed by Google.

00:39:43.700 --> 00:39:47.840
So once it hit that layer, after
that it hit the specific test

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:50.335
project, where we
parked a domain.

00:39:50.335 --> 00:39:52.460
We didn't want it to have
anything to do with eBay.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:54.127
We just wanted to be
something separate.

00:39:54.127 --> 00:39:56.720
That way we didn't have any
liability if anything ever

00:39:56.720 --> 00:39:57.870
happened to us.

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:02.000
But what it was, it was a Cloud
DNS Managed Zone externally.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.870
We had a static IP assigned
to the load balance fit

00:40:04.870 --> 00:40:07.190
and attached a less encrypt
managed certificate with it

00:40:07.190 --> 00:40:08.660
that automatically rotated.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.690
And then it did our
SSL off-loading for us.

00:40:10.690 --> 00:40:13.940
So the next hop would then
be the Reblaze proxy tier.

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:17.240
So this Reblaze proxy tier
was managed instance group,

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:19.460
which use an instance
template of a managed

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:21.410
image through Reblaze.

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:25.580
It was using N1
standard 16 instance

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:28.910
types, which is 16 VCPUs
and 60 gigs of RAM each.

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:30.710
And it autoscaled
based off of demand.

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:33.140
So in reality, for
the test, we just

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:34.910
kept two instances
running at all times.

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:37.077
And during the testing, it
would autoscale based off

00:40:37.077 --> 00:40:39.320
of the specific metrics
we had provisioned for it.

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:43.340
And then we had the BigQuery--
the logs go to BigQuery.

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:46.250
So it was a structured data
set that was IM-controlled.

00:40:46.250 --> 00:40:47.540
We had access to them.

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:48.260
Nobody else did.

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:49.885
And that was part of
the reasons why we

00:40:49.885 --> 00:40:51.770
wanted to do this experiment.

00:40:51.770 --> 00:40:54.920
We wanted to maintain some
kind of control of the data

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:57.290
that we were actually creating.

00:40:57.290 --> 00:40:59.870
From there, it would
forward it to another cloud

00:40:59.870 --> 00:41:02.840
DNS-managed zone that
was considered internal.

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:06.920
And then it sent it to
this specific tier of VMs

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:07.820
that we provisioned.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:13.070
These are vulnerable Docker
instances that we created.

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:15.710
There were susceptible things,
like OS top 10 attacks.

00:41:15.710 --> 00:41:18.080
The idea is that we wanted
to keep them to the point

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:19.163
where we could break them.

00:41:19.163 --> 00:41:22.510
So if anything penetrated
here, we wanted to see--

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:25.790
monitor this
specific tier and see

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:28.190
if anything failed within
the upper part of the stack.

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:33.940
So we partnered up with a third
party DDoS company, specialized

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:36.490
in kind of test driving what
your security controls are,

00:41:36.490 --> 00:41:38.230
your DDoS mitigation
solutions are,

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:41.337
to simulate the true scale
of distributed attack.

00:41:41.337 --> 00:41:43.420
And we wanted to focus on
specific DDoS types that

00:41:43.420 --> 00:41:46.630
were considered common
types from layer 3

00:41:46.630 --> 00:41:48.430
to layer 7 for our test cycle.

00:41:48.430 --> 00:41:51.070
So the layer 3, layer 4 attacks
listed were more focused

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:53.270
on megabits per second.

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:55.060
As an example, like
a UDP flood, which

00:41:55.060 --> 00:41:58.030
uses UDP data gram
containing IP packets

00:41:58.030 --> 00:42:00.610
to flood random ports
on a target network.

00:42:00.610 --> 00:42:04.780
Or, well, the layer 7 attacks
were more connection-focused--

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:07.150
connection per second-focused,
like HTTP flood,

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:09.370
which is an attack that
use a large number of HTTP

00:42:09.370 --> 00:42:13.160
get or post requests to target
an application or web server.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:16.570
So the sequence of events
was the vendor initiated

00:42:16.570 --> 00:42:18.460
each attack individually.

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:21.820
During each attack we searched
for impacts to availability

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:25.150
through a series of scripted
curl requests and web page

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:28.413
reloads and then monitored
the performance on the VMs.

00:42:28.413 --> 00:42:29.830
And then following
each attack, we

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:32.770
cleared out any
dynamic IP blacklists

00:42:32.770 --> 00:42:37.000
before the next attack started.

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:39.220
So here are the results
of the test as follows.

00:42:39.220 --> 00:42:41.500
So a pass means
good in our case.

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:44.720
So no visible impact to any
of the backend service VMs.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:46.657
And a fail would mean
there was visible impact

00:42:46.657 --> 00:42:47.740
to the backend service VM.

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:50.440
So as expected,
all layer 3 attacks

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:53.290
were mitigated at the Google
front-end via Cloud Armor.

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:55.450
Any of the non-TCP
traffic was discarded

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:57.780
by Google's
distributed firewall.

00:42:57.780 --> 00:43:00.550
And this left literally only the
TCP connection floods and TCP

00:43:00.550 --> 00:43:02.812
flag attacks that could
reach the front-end VIP.

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:07.153
So here are some
of our findings.

00:43:07.153 --> 00:43:08.570
This is kind of
like the feedback.

00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:12.130
Mind you, when we did this test,
we did this late last year,

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.380
we worked really well with
Google to say, hey, listen,

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:15.720
this is some of what we found.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:17.470
And some of the product
that you see today

00:43:17.470 --> 00:43:19.170
and that was actually
released at RSA

00:43:19.170 --> 00:43:21.000
is from some of the
feedback that we

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:22.960
were able to give them
to make the product

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:24.220
a lot more resilient.

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:28.538
So at the time, all the
attacks were silently dropped.

00:43:28.538 --> 00:43:30.580
So we really didn't get
any visibility into that.

00:43:30.580 --> 00:43:33.483
We didn't really have the rich
logs that we wanted to see.

00:43:33.483 --> 00:43:35.400
Ideally, you want to be
able to correlate that

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:37.330
across all your properties.

00:43:37.330 --> 00:43:40.560
So you weren't able to do things
like short term log analysis,

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:44.620
or short term threat analysis
or long term threat analysis.

00:43:44.620 --> 00:43:46.620
And that's kind of some
of the feedback we gave.

00:43:46.620 --> 00:43:48.330
We wanted to see
some kind of summary

00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:51.635
logs so that we could be aware
of any anomalous activity that

00:43:51.635 --> 00:43:53.640
was happening in Google,
and at the same time,

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:55.807
so we could start correlating
that and training that

00:43:55.807 --> 00:43:57.021
across all of our properties.

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:01.820
So as you can see, all
the layer 7 attacks

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:04.310
were mitigated within Reblaze,
either through dynamic rule

00:44:04.310 --> 00:44:05.630
or bot challenges.

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:08.690
And following each test, we
needed to clear the dynamic IP

00:44:08.690 --> 00:44:11.160
blacklist so that
the next set of tests

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:14.130
could successfully run.

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:15.620
And from a visibility
perspective,

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:17.630
again, some of the things
that we encountered

00:44:17.630 --> 00:44:20.120
is that some of the load
balance logs provided

00:44:20.120 --> 00:44:22.370
some rudimentary access
logs and not a form

00:44:22.370 --> 00:44:24.470
that was easy to analyze.

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:28.400
And at the time, they only
contained IP blacklist logging.

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:30.800
From the Reblaze
perspective, all the data

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:31.905
was through BigQuery.

00:44:31.905 --> 00:44:33.530
Some of the canned
table visualizations

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:34.400
were difficult to pivot.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:35.750
We gave that feedback
to Reblaze and they

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:36.920
made them a lot better.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:39.080
And again, since everything
is logged to BigQuery,

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:40.970
you can kind of build
your own analysis tools

00:44:40.970 --> 00:44:43.970
if you want to for visibility.

00:44:43.970 --> 00:44:46.290
These are enabled
defenses that we had.

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:48.580
So the global ACL is a dynamic--

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:49.970
it's a network-based ACL.

00:44:49.970 --> 00:44:55.250
It does things like IP blocking,
ASM blocking, or via country.

00:44:55.250 --> 00:44:56.920
The two that we
primarily used was

00:44:56.920 --> 00:45:01.220
the global DR XDeny, which
is a dynamic IP blacklist,

00:45:01.220 --> 00:45:03.410
and the Deny Bot.

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:06.080
Deny Anonymous Proxies and
Deny Tor are self-explanatory.

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:08.210
Those lists are
automatically updated for us.

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:10.730
But a Deny Bot is interesting,
because a lot of vendors,

00:45:10.730 --> 00:45:13.030
what they normally
do is their response

00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:14.420
is just injected JavaScript.

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:16.565
But we found Reblaze to
be a lot more intelligent,

00:45:16.565 --> 00:45:19.328
where it takes a lot of
human detection components

00:45:19.328 --> 00:45:21.620
in order to be able to detect
that it's actually a bot.

00:45:21.620 --> 00:45:24.530
So some of the techniques
were environment validation,

00:45:24.530 --> 00:45:27.068
proof of work, human
behavior interaction,

00:45:27.068 --> 00:45:28.610
which is literally
like mouse clicks,

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:31.020
moves, and scrolls to
validate connection.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.380
And then we had a
default eBay WAF policy,

00:45:33.380 --> 00:45:36.830
which is where we whitelisted
HTTP methods, GET and POST,

00:45:36.830 --> 00:45:38.450
and denying all
others, that way we

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:42.360
can allow certain arguments in
while denying everything else.

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:44.748
So what you see here are
the Reblaze traffic graphs.

00:45:44.748 --> 00:45:46.790
And this is the graph of
the scripted attack test

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:48.530
before, after, and during.

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:51.913
On the y-axis you see--

00:45:51.913 --> 00:45:53.580
it represents the
connections per second

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:55.163
while the bottom
shows the time range.

00:45:55.163 --> 00:45:59.490
And so this is a scripted
HTTP-based web flood.

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:01.973
And so you can see
that Reblaze starts

00:46:01.973 --> 00:46:03.890
by challenging each
request and asking the bot

00:46:03.890 --> 00:46:05.333
to re-request the same page.

00:46:05.333 --> 00:46:07.250
Since the bot never
re-requests the same page,

00:46:07.250 --> 00:46:09.530
its request is not
passed through.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:12.710
And these bots get caught by
the web page dynamic rules,

00:46:12.710 --> 00:46:14.420
and then they're
temporary blacklist.

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:16.280
And again, you can kind of see
the trending from the bandwidth

00:46:16.280 --> 00:46:17.120
as well.

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:19.490
So I start sending challenges,
the bandwidth increased.

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:23.600
And as soon as the bots are
moved to the dynamic blacklist,

00:46:23.600 --> 00:46:24.880
the bandwidth drops.

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:26.420
And as Emil
mentioned previously,

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:28.878
these dynamic blacklists can
be configured to auto-escalate

00:46:28.878 --> 00:46:31.070
to Cloud Armor.

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:33.158
And again, this is the
simulated browser attack.

00:46:33.158 --> 00:46:35.450
And we had some connection
[INAUDIBLE] during the test.

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:37.042
But as with the
scripted attacks,

00:46:37.042 --> 00:46:38.750
you can see that the
same thing went out.

00:46:38.750 --> 00:46:40.910
The challenges went out.

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:43.970
And as soon as challenging
the response several times,

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:46.220
the bots got moved into
a dynamic blacklist.

00:46:46.220 --> 00:46:48.980
And again, the same with
each-- with the bandwidth

00:46:48.980 --> 00:46:49.850
to Reblaze proxy.

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:55.540
So the verdict for
us, it was a pass.

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:57.730
Cloud Armor provides
scaled out defenses

00:46:57.730 --> 00:46:59.350
against volumetric DDoS attacks.

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.410
And Google's reputation
precedes itself

00:47:02.410 --> 00:47:04.930
with mitigating these
type of attacks.

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.720
There was a few different--
the blips that we found

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:09.693
is that some of the
telemetry, but given

00:47:09.693 --> 00:47:11.110
the feedback we
were able to give,

00:47:11.110 --> 00:47:13.490
we see that in the
product that is today.

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:18.100
And again, with Reblaze's bot
detection and dynamic rules,

00:47:18.100 --> 00:47:22.090
it was amazing at being able
to stop a lot of the layer 7

00:47:22.090 --> 00:47:23.860
attacks that we encountered.

00:47:23.860 --> 00:47:26.162
At the same time, from the
visibility perspective,

00:47:26.162 --> 00:47:28.120
we were actually able to
get a lot of telemetry

00:47:28.120 --> 00:47:29.460
that we wanted.

00:47:29.460 --> 00:47:33.250
So overall, Google
Cloud Armor, Reblaze

00:47:33.250 --> 00:47:35.620
were a sound solution for
DDoS attacks of all kinds.

00:47:35.620 --> 00:47:37.964
And that's that.

00:47:37.964 --> 00:47:41.268
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:42.095 --> 00:47:43.970
Also, we have-- there's
a survey on your app.

00:47:43.970 --> 00:47:45.370
So if you could,
please take some time

00:47:45.370 --> 00:47:46.300
to give some feedback.

00:47:46.300 --> 00:47:48.400
And thank you.

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:50.500
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:54.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

